brA(a,b,c){var s,r=b.toLowerCase(),q=c==null,p=q?null:c.b
if(p==null)p="this topic"
s=A.b92(c)
if(a===B.Mp){q=q?null:c.b
if(q==null)q="your notes"
return"Let's do a quick retrieval check on **"+q+"** \u2014 no peeking.\n\n**Question:** "+A.bq9(c)+"\n\nGive me your answer and a one-sentence reason. I'll tell you what's strong and what to revisit.\n"}if(a===B.Mq){q=q?null:c.b
if(q==null)q="Your notes"
return"## "+q+" \u2014 quick summary\n\nHere's the compressed version I'd put on a single revision card:\n\n- **Core idea:** "+A.bqE(c)+"\n- **Mechanism:** focus on the causal chain, not isolated definitions.\n- **Exam move:** be ready to compare it with a neighboring concept and give one concrete example.\n\n**Three things to test yourself on**\n1. Explain the main process from memory.\n2. Draw or label the key structure.\n3. Connect it to a real-world consequence.\n\n> "+s.e+"\n\n[Source: "+s.c+", p. "+s.d+"]\n"}if(B.c.n(r,"mitochondria")||B.c.n(r,"atp"))return"The mitochondrion is the cell's **ATP factory**, and the key is the proton gradient.\n\n### What happens\n\n1. The electron transport chain pumps protons from the matrix into the intermembrane space.\n2. That creates an electrochemical gradient \u2014 a form of stored potential energy.\n3. Protons flow back through **ATP synthase**, which drives ADP + Pi \u2192 ATP.\n\nSo the mitochondrion doesn't \"make energy\" from nothing; it converts the chemical energy in NADH and FADH\u2082 into a usable gradient, then into ATP.\n\n> "+s.e+"\n\n[Source: "+s.c+", p. "+s.d+"]\n\n**Check yourself:** why does an uncoupling agent like DNP increase oxygen consumption but stop ATP production?\n"
if(B.c.n(r,"photosynthesis")||B.c.n(r,"calvin"))return"Photosynthesis has two linked stages \u2014 and exams love the connection.\n\n### 1. Light reactions\n- Happen in the **thylakoid membrane**\n- Split water, release O\u2082, and produce **ATP + NADPH**\n\n### 2. Calvin cycle\n- Happens in the **stroma**\n- Uses ATP and NADPH to fix CO\u2082 into G3P\n- Rubisco is the key enzyme\n\nThe elegant part: the light reactions make the energy carriers that the Calvin cycle spends. If light stops, the Calvin cycle stalls once the ATP/NADPH pool is exhausted.\n\n> "+s.e+"\n\n[Source: "+s.c+", p. "+s.d+"]\n\nWant to test this with a compare-and-contrast question?\n"
if(B.c.n(r,"memory")||B.c.n(r,"hippocampus")||B.c.n(r,"ltp"))return"Think of memory in three operations: **encoding \u2192 storage \u2192 retrieval**.\n\n### The cellular story\nRepeated stimulation of a synapse can produce **long-term potentiation (LTP)**: more AMPA receptors, stronger responses, and a more excitable connection. The hippocampus is a key hub for forming new episodic memories, but it isn't the place where all long-term memories live.\n\n### Why forgetting happens\n- **Decay** over time\n- **Interference** from similar material\n- **Retrieval failure** when cues don't match the encoding context\n\n> "+s.e+"\n\n[Source: "+s.c+", p. "+s.d+"]\n\nA strong exam answer connects LTP to a behavioral example. Can you give me one?\n"
if(B.c.n(r,"integral")||B.c.n(r,"integration")||B.c.n(r,"calculus"))return"Integration by parts is the product rule run backwards:\n\n$$\\int u\\,dv = uv - \\int v\\,du$$\n\n### How to choose u\nUse **LIATE**: Logarithmic, Inverse trig, Algebraic, Trigonometric, Exponential. Pick the earlier category as **u**, because you want it to become simpler after differentiating.\n\n### Worked shape\nFor \u222bx\xb7e\u02e3 dx, choose u = x and dv = e\u02e3 dx:\n- du = dx, v = e\u02e3\n- Result: x\xb7e\u02e3 \u2212 \u222be\u02e3 dx = x\xb7e\u02e3 \u2212 e\u02e3 + C\n\n> "+s.e+"\n\n[Source: "+s.c+", p. "+s.d+"]\n\nTry \u222bx\xb7cos(x) dx and tell me your u and dv.\n"
return"Let's make **"+p+"** concrete.\n\n### Start with the structure\nBreak the question into three parts: what is happening, what causes it, and how you would recognize it on an exam.\n\n### A useful study move\nExplain the idea out loud in 60 seconds without notes. Then compare your explanation to the source. The gap is your next review target.\n\n> "+s.e+"\n\n[Source: "+s.c+", p. "+s.d+"]\n\nIf you paste the exact question or upload the relevant pages, I can ground the answer more tightly in your notes.\n"},
